        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="Hn 13.12" corresp="#wit-lat"><pb n="558–559" ed="#liebermann"/>Qui<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="y" target="#Hn_13-12.lnote.oe.y"/> in bello<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="zh" target="#Hn_13-12.lnote.oe.zh"/> campali<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="52" target="#Hn_13-12.lnote.oe.52"/> uel nauali pro timiditate fugiet a domino uel a socio suo, perdat omne<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="53" target="#Hn_13-12.lnote.oe.53"/> quod habet et uitam<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Hn_13-12.lnote.oe.1"/> suam, et manus mittat dominus ad peccuniam<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="a" target="#Hn_13-12.lnote.oe.a"/> suam et terram quam ei dedit; et si bocland<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="a" target="#Hn_13-12.lnote.oe.a"/> habet, in ius regium cadat.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">New row (October 2026): Liebermann’s text transcribed from Gesetze I p. 558–559 (two independent readings of the page images, differences settled against the page)</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_13-12.lnote.oe.y" n="y" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">558</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">vgl.</hi> 10, 1</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">cf. 10, 1</seg></note>
